WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:03.679
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.679 --> 00:00:10.410
If we have an animation movie or a computer
game where, like in any other digital medium,

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:16.100
we wish to have high-quality, lifelike animations
for our characters, we likely have to use

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:17.250
motion capture.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:22.100
Motion capture means that we put an actor
in the studio, and we ask this person to perform

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:27.330
cartwheels and other motion types that we
wish to transfer to our virtual characters.

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:33.179
This works really well, but recording and
cleaning all this data is a very expensive

00:00:33.179 --> 00:00:35.000
and laborious process.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:40.930
As we are entering the age of AI, of course,
I wonder if there is a better way to do this?

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:45.269
Just think about it...we have no shortage
of videos here on the Youtube about people

00:00:45.269 --> 00:00:50.199
performing cartwheels and other moves, and
we have a bunch of learning algorithms that

00:00:50.199 --> 00:00:54.100
know what pose they are taking during the
video.

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:57.390
Surely we can make something happen here,
right?

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:59.550
Well, yes, and no.

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:05.159
A few methods already exist to perform this,
but all of them have dealbreaking drawbacks.

00:01:05.159 --> 00:01:10.390
For instance, this previous work predicts
the body poses for each frame, but each of

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:16.190
them have small individual inaccuracies that
produce this annoying flickering effect.

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:20.060
Researchers like to refer to this as the lack
of temporal coherence.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:23.720
But, this new technique is able to remedy
this.

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:25.590
Great result!

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:30.720
This new work also boasts a long list of other
incredible improvements.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:35.530
For instance, the resulting motions are also
simulated in a virtual environment, and it

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:41.299
is shown that they are quite robust - so much
so, that we can throw a bunch of boxes against

00:01:41.299 --> 00:01:48.090
the AI, and it still can adjust to it.

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:49.200
Kind of.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.700
These motions can be retargeted to different
body shapes.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:57.670
You can see as it is demonstrated here quite
aptly with this neat little nod to Boston

00:01:57.670 --> 00:01:58.820
Dynamics.

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:10.259
It can also adapt to challenging new environments,
or, get this, it can even work from a single

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:15.819
photo instead of a video by completing the
motion seen within.

00:02:15.819 --> 00:02:18.090
What kind of wizardry is that?

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:19.890
How could it possibly perform that?

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:20.980
It works the following way.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:25.490
First, we take an input photo or video, and
perform pose estimation on it.

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:29.640
But this is still a per-frame computation,
and you remember that this doesn't give us

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:31.580
temporal consistency.

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:37.019
This motion reconstruction step ensures that
we have smooth transitions between the poses.

00:02:37.019 --> 00:02:43.110
And now comes the best part: we start simulating
a virtual environment, where a digital character

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:47.130
tries to move its body parts to perform these
actions.

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:52.659
If we do this, we can not only reproduce these
motions, but also continue them.

00:02:52.659 --> 00:02:54.659
This is where the wizardry lies.

00:02:54.659 --> 00:03:00.299
If you read the paper, which you should absolutely
do, you will see that it uses OpenAI's amazing

00:03:00.299 --> 00:03:05.720
Proximal Policy Optimization algorithm to
find the best motions.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:06.720
Absolutely amazing.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:11.370
So this can perform and complete a variety
of motions,

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:16.769
adapts to more challenging landscapes, and
do all this in a temporally smooth manner.

00:03:16.769 --> 00:03:20.680
However, the Gangnam style dance still proves
to be too hard.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:22.690
The technology is not there yet.

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:26.160
We also thank Insilico Medicine for supporting
this video.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:29.680
They work on AI-based drug discovery and aging
research.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:33.140
They have some unbelievable papers on these
topics.

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:36.859
Make sure to check them out, and this paper
as well in the video description.

00:03:36.859 --> 00:03:40.260
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

